The archived incident calls every merged date report one parser bug. The executable KB views
separate the reports by tenant locale, booking form and channel, rather than the intake tag or
subject. The table below computes the current export's counts after deduplicating report IDs.

<Callout>
Keep one ticket per cause. The shared month-first parser and the booking-v3 date hint need
separate fixes and separate verification. Neither is marked fixed by this reassessment.
</Callout>

## What the reports support

The cause-specific work is filed as [shared parser, 379 reports](https://github.com/felipemichelin/mega-course-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/1)
and [booking-v3 hint, 33 reports](https://github.com/felipemichelin/mega-course-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/2).
The table's ticket column describes the original export, before these new tickets were filed.

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

### Shared parser reads month first

For a day-first tenant, the shared parser reads the date month first. For example, an app
report from Harbour Dental says 7 April became 4 July (R-41200); another reverses 4 July and
7 April (R-41201). Fixing this path requires an explicit date contract at the boundary and
regression checks for each affected channel. Intake tags are a guess, not the cause.

### Booking-v3 prints a month-first hint for day-first tenants

In the form channel, booking-v3 displays `MM/DD/YYYY` while the form reads the date in the
tenant's day-first order. These users follow the displayed instruction and still get a wrong
date. Fix the hint and parsing contract together; retest day-first tenants through booking-v3.
This cause must not disappear inside the shared-parser ticket.

### Uncertain symptom reports stay visible

Some reports describe a one-day move rather than a clear month/day swap. The component's
footnotes identify how many are filed by tenant. Preserve those reports for follow-up: ask
for the input value, timezone, expected date and actual date. Their wording alone does not
prove a third root cause, and filing them by tenant is not proof of an individual diagnosis.

## When the desk received reports and when it merged them

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

Receipt time and merge time are separate events. A late merge does not date the original
incident. The chart computes both series from the source records.

## Triage and knowledge changes proposed

Read the tenant's locale, form and channel before treating two reports as duplicates. Check
the report ID first so overlapping export pages do not inflate counts. A matching subject
does not prove a matching cause; a different subject does not prove a different cause.

The accepted decision 0001 remains intact as history. Proposed decision 0002 describes the
replacement rule. The original #4471 page remains the historical claim this reassessment
corrects. Recompute this page against a fresh desk export before relying on it for live triage.

## Evidence and limits

This page runs `CauseTable`, `QueueTimeline`, tenant lookup and `causes.ts` from the supplied
KB export. It does not assert the live queue has stopped changing. The prepared issue drafts
carry the computed counts, report IDs, source paths and verification criteria. The repository
README links the cause-specific GitHub tickets and records their filing status.

